WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:02.810
THE RISE OF ZELLE:
I’m sure you’re all familiar with Venmo

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:06.880
and PayPal, but have you heard of Zelle?

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.360
Zelle was only launched in June of 2017, but
they’ve been putting PayPal to shame.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:19.210
In fact, they’ve nearly grown to half of
PayPal’s size within just these 5 years.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.250
Currently, PayPal handles just over $300 billion
worth of payments every quarter.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.699
Meanwhile, Zelle has already reached over
$150 billion per quarter.

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:34.160
Something else to note is that the average
Zelle transaction is worth a lot more than

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.610
the average PayPal transaction.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:43.360
Last year, PayPal completed a total of 19.3
billion transactions which translates to $1.25

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.380
trillion.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:48.340
This means that each transaction was worth
$65 on average.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:53.540
Meanwhile, Zelle has completed over 5 billion
transactions within the past 5 years which

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.230
translates to nearly $1.5 trillion.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:01.399
This means that each transaction was worth
nearly $300 on average.

00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:07.430
So, not only has Zelle been exploding in popularity,
but it appears to be the app of choice for

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:08.900
larger payments.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:14.830
At this rate, it seems like Zelle overtaking
PayPal is inevitable, but how was a new player

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:19.250
able to put an established giant to shame?

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:24.950
EARLY FAILURE:
One of the main reasons for the popularity

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.110
of Zelle is the players behind it.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:32.060
Zelle isn’t some startup like Stripe or
Affirm, it’s actually the brainchild of

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.060
the largest financial institutions in the
world.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:41.020
This includes Bank of America, Capital One,
JPMorgan Chase, PNC Bank, Truist, US Bank,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.170
and Wells Fargo.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.780
With such large names behind, you might be
thinking that the success of Zelle is a no

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.060
brainer, but that’s not actually the case.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.079
In fact, Zelle was not their first attempt
at taking on PayPal.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:59.340
Their first attempt was actually a service
called clearXchange which came out in April

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.860
of 2011.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:06.229
The project was initially launched by Bank
of America, JP Morgan Chase, and Wells Fargo,

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:11.550
and it was based on the same ideology as Zelle,
eliminating the friction and cost associated

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.380
with making payments.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.379
Instead of having to use account numbers and
routing numbers, you could just use someone’s

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:19.260
email address or phone number.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:25.420
But, despite the promising pitch, clearXchange
didn’t translate all that well in the real

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:26.420
world.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.420
For starters, their branding was garbage.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.460
One of the key characteristics that make PayPal
and Venmo so popular is that they’ve become

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:32.460
verbs.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.970
It’s common for people say “I can PayPal
you” or “I can Venmo you”.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:41.110
But, ain’t no one gonna be saying “I can
clearXchange you”.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:45.190
And given that much of these transactions
take place amongst younger individuals in

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:48.650
social settings, public perception is critical.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:54.500
Aside from having terrible branding, clearXchange
was also rather clunky and slow.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:59.810
It was painfully evident that clearXchange
was not designed by a modern organziation.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.049
To initiate transfers, you had to navigate
to portals on these banks’ websites and

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:05.190
apps.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:10.460
And if you used any of these portals 10 years
ago, you know how clunky the UI was.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.300
It wasn’t just the UI that was clunky either.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.260
The payments themselves were just as clunky
as it took up to five business days for transactions

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.360
to be completed.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.120
Do you know what else takes up to five business
days to complete?

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:24.930
Traditional ACH transfers.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:29.650
It appeared that these banks had just thrown
together a platform that could initiate ACH

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.830
transfers using just someone’s email address
or phone number.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:36.310
But, nothing else about the service stuck
out.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:40.569
Something else that we should note is that
clearXchange offered no real benefit to most

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:41.569
users.

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:45.150
One of their largest selling features was
that there were no fees to send money.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:50.030
But, the problem is that PayPal nor Venmo
charge any fees to send money to friends or

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.240
family either.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.060
So, really there was no reason to use clearXchange.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:00.409
From the average person’s perspective, Venmo
was already popular amongst their friend groups,

00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:05.750
transfers took less than 5 days to complete,
the UI was magnitudes better, and as far as

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.959
they were concerned, it was free.

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:12.400
So, there was really no incentive to make
the jump to clearXchange.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.259
At first, the banks thought that the reason
for the failure was a lack of participating

00:04:16.259 --> 00:04:17.259
members.

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:22.280
So, they worked on growing the size of their
network, but eventually they realized that

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.840
what clearXchange needed wasn’t more fundamental
features.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.440
What it needed was a facelift, and that brings
us to the introduction of Zelle.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.070
INTRODUCING ZELLE:
The first problem that Zelle addressed was

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.430
the branding shortfall.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:41.900
They changed the name from clearXchange to
Zelle.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:48.210
Zelle was not only easier to pronounce than
Venmo, but it was unique and memorable as

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.070
it started with a Z.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:53.680
The old branding wasn’t the only thing that
the banks left behind.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.000
They also more or less ditched their institutional
business.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.590
Remember how we discussed that clearXchange
was free?

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:05.479
Well, it wasn’t just cost effective for
individual users, it was also cost effective

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:08.630
for institutional users unlike PayPal and
Venmo.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:14.070
So, the banks were very much hoping that businesses
and governments would start using the service

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:17.009
and that this would prompt consumers to use
it as well.

00:05:17.009 --> 00:05:24.170
But, this is rarely how things work out, it’s
almost always the other way around.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.030
Institutions usually shift to use what their
customers want.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:32.690
This is precisely why businesses like PayPal,
Visa, and Mastercard are so successful.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:36.780
Consumers want to use them and businesses
have to pay the price.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.431
ClearXchange was looking to eliminate the
price for businesses, but that doesn’t make

00:05:40.431 --> 00:05:43.470
the service any more popular amongst consumers.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:47.950
They eventually realized this with Zelle and
they pretty much ditched the services market

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:48.979
altogether.

00:05:48.979 --> 00:05:53.970
Instead, Zelle is only intended for paying
people that you already know and trust.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:59.639
In fact, Zelle disclaims any responsibility
for goods and services that are bought through

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:00.639
the system.

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:04.290
While this might sound like a disadvantage,
this is what actually allows for Zelle’s

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:05.290
biggest selling feature.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:10.110
By keeping the checking and verifications
to a minimum, Zelle was able to allow for

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:11.509
instant transfers.

00:06:11.509 --> 00:06:17.100
This feature was actually launched under clearXchange,
but Zelle is what became known for it.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.870
This was really Zelle’s checkmate move because
this was something that PayPal simply couldn’t

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.310
compete with, atleast not very easily.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:29.630
PayPal would essentially have to become a
bank if they wanted to allow for instant transfers.

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:34.599
PayPal has very much moved in this direction,
but they still haven’t been able to eliminate

00:06:34.599 --> 00:06:36.500
instant tranfer fees altogether.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:41.300
Currently, they charge 1.75% for instant transfers.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.890
Something else that makes Zelle especially
appealing is that you don’t really have

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:46.940
to create an account to use it.

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:51.120
Virtually everyone already has a bank account
with a Zelle participant.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.460
So, all you have to do is enable the Zelle
feature within your bank account.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:59.900
This also means that payments go directly
to your bank account, so you don’t have

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:04.070
to deal with nominal sums of money just laying
around in your PayPal account.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:09.729
To be honest, none of these are groundbreaking
changes from clearXchange, but the facts don’t

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:14.319
lie as Zelle is magnitudes more successful
than clearXchange.

00:07:14.319 --> 00:07:19.320
It just goes to show that how you’re offering
a service is just as important if not more

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.979
important than what you’re offering itself.

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:26.980
The banks didn't realize this a decade ago,
but with Zelle they’re more aware than ever

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:29.902
which has given PayPal and Venmo a run for
thie rmoney.

00:07:29.902 --> 00:07:37.460
THE BAD:
Hearing all this, Zelle may sound like the

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:42.790
perfect option for money transfers, but like
always, there’s another side to the story

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:43.900
as well.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:49.260
For one, Zelle doesn’t offer any sort of
consumer or seller protection which makes

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.370
it virtually useless for small businesses
and merchants.

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:57.990
Now, we should note that PayPal’s services
within this realm aren’t that great.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:03.720
In fact, it’s actually terrible and it seems
like PayPal almost always sides with the scammer

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:07.190
regardless of whether the scammer is a customer
or a merchant.

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:11.830
And this is why PayPal has a trustpilot review
of just 1.3.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:18.440
But, with that being said, atleast with PayPal,
there is a system, with Zelle, the system

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:20.250
is nonexistent.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:25.310
This is likely something that Zelle is working
on in the background, but as of right now,

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:29.690
if you want some sort of transaction protection,
you’re gonna have to go with PayPal.

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:33.300
Not only will you not have transaction protection
with Zelle, but you’ll also be more prone

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.300
to scammers.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:39.310
Here’s the thing, Zelle offers instant transfer
directly into anyone’s bank account.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.289
That’s like a scammers dream right there.

00:08:42.289 --> 00:08:46.720
And let’s just say scammers have been hard
at work to exploit Zelle.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:50.360
Their latest scam tactic is known as the Pay
Yourself Scam.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:55.570
The scam starts with a text message that alerts
you that your bank account may be at risk.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:59.910
If you respond to this message, the scammer
will pretend to be a bank representative and

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.910
will ask for a one-time verification passcode
to secure your account.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:07.940
What they’re actually doing though is enrolling
their bank account through Zelle using your

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:09.810
phone number and email.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:13.750
This means that the next time someone sends
you money through Zelle, it goes directly

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:15.410
to the scammer’s bank account.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:21.290
And with the average Zelle transaction clocking
in at nearly $300, this become quite a big

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:22.290
issue.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:26.150
The banks are looking to stand behind Zelle
and have been refunding these transactions.

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:32.060
But, this has been quite expensive, $255 million
expensive to be exact.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:37.991
Now, you could argue that this amount is nothing
in comparison to banks’ net income, and

00:09:37.991 --> 00:09:39.120
that’s true.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:45.769
But, here’s the thing, Zelle doesn’t produce
any revenue whatsoever, and there’s no clear

00:09:45.769 --> 00:09:51.520
path to generate revenue moving forward either,
so it’s kind of hard to justify these expenses.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:56.370
I mean they’re basically spending all this
money just to protect their marketshare.

00:09:56.370 --> 00:10:01.670
Even that argument is a bit iffy given that
it’s not like people are opening Wells Fargo

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:03.800
accounts just to use Zelle.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:08.779
Really, the only thing that Zelle is accomplishing
for these big banks is preventing PayPal from

00:10:08.779 --> 00:10:10.980
gaining too much power against them.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:15.370
For larger banks like Chase and Wells Fargo,
that’s probably a worthwhile investment,

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:19.100
but the same cannot be said about smaller
banks.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:23.340
It appears that these institutions will be
kicked out of the system if they don’t agree

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:28.680
to reimburse victims, so it looks like they’ll
have no choice but to participate.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:34.990
All of this probably sounds like a good thing
for consumers, but is it?

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:41.050
THE UGLY:
While Zelle is truly an exceptional service,

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:44.250
all of this brings us back to ground zero.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:49.040
One of the biggest appeals of using alternative
financial institutions is that it takes power

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:50.480
away from the big banks.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:54.029
But, embracing Zelle does the exact opposite.

00:10:54.029 --> 00:10:59.589
Not only will the big banks have control over
our savings, but they’ll also have control

00:10:59.589 --> 00:11:01.450
over our transactions.

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:06.720
And at this point, nothing short of a black
swan event could stifle the growth Zelle.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.620
If the banks launch some sort of transaction
protection further down the line, this will

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:13.430
simply make Zelle even more appealing.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:17.520
Something else to note is that Zelle has a
much better shot internationally than PayPal

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.899
ever did thanks to the members behind it.

00:11:19.899 --> 00:11:25.390
UBS, for example, would likely have a much
harder time turning down Chase, Wells Fargo,

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:30.269
Bank of America, and basically the rest of
the US banking system, than just turning down

00:11:30.269 --> 00:11:31.300
PayPal.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:35.480
In the end, Zelle’s popularity is really
not surprising.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:39.790
The big banks are basically just flexing their
financial power by offering an phenomenal

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:41.769
service for free.

00:11:41.769 --> 00:11:45.690
While they did have some struggles in the
early days, they’ve more than figured those

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:48.880
out, and now they’re looking to overtake
PayPal.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:51.930
But, that’s just the first step.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:56.510
It appears that the real goal of Zelle is
to eventually challenge Visa, Mastercard,

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:58.209
and American Express.

00:11:58.209 --> 00:12:03.279
For decades, banks have been reliant on these
payment processors to complete merchant transactions,

00:12:03.279 --> 00:12:05.889
but Zelle could change all of that.

00:12:05.889 --> 00:12:10.940
And based on Jamie Dimon’s pay by bank vision,
it looks like this is very much where the

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:14.540
industry is heading, but that’s just what
I think.

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:15.610
Are you a fan of Zelle?

